<!--
doc/src/sgml/ref/cypher_finish.sgml
PostgreSQL documentation
-->

<refentry id="cypher-finish">
 <indexterm zone="cypher-finish">
  <primary>FINISH</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>FINISH</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>Cypher - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>FINISH</refname>
  <refpurpose>end a query without projecting a result</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
FINISH
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-finish-description">
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   Every query has to end with <command>RETURN</command>, <command>FINISH</command> or a write
   clause. <command>FINISH</command> is the ending that produces no result. It takes no
   arguments, projects no columns and closes the statement.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>FINISH</command> has to be the last clause. Anything after it raises
   <literal>FINISH must be at the end of the query</literal>.
  </para>

  <para>
   After a read, <command>FINISH</command> discards the rows. The planner applies it as
   <literal>LIMIT 0</literal>, so the scan is opened but no row is fetched. After a write, the
   write still happens. <command>FINISH</command> only suppresses the projection.
  </para>

  <para>
   A query that ends in <command>FINISH</command> returns an empty result, with no columns and
   no rows. <application>psql</application> prints it as a <literal>--</literal> header
   followed by <literal>(0 rows)</literal>, and <command>\gdesc</command> reports
   <literal>The command has no result, or the result has no columns.</literal> A write that
   ends in <command>FINISH</command> reports its ordinary command tag instead of a result, such
   as <literal>UPDATE 1</literal> for one created vertex.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-finish-parameters">
  <title>Parameters</title>

  <para>
   <command>FINISH</command> takes no parameters.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-finish-examples">
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   These examples use the graph in <xref linkend="agensgraph-example-data"/>.
  </para>

  <para>
   Run a read for its plan and nothing else. There is no Cypher spelling of this. A Cypher
   query with no <command>RETURN</command> and no write clause is rejected:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie) FINISH;

--
(0 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Perform a write without reading the result back. This is the form to reach for when the
   created elements should not be shipped to the client:
<programlisting>
CREATE (:movie {title: 'Tenet', year: 2020, genre: 'Action'}) FINISH;

UPDATE 1
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   The vertex is there:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'Tenet'}) RETURN m.title AS film, m.year AS year;

  film   | year
---------+------
 "Tenet" | 2020
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   The plan of a read shows the <literal>LIMIT 0</literal> the clause compiles to, above the
   scan it never draws from:
<programlisting>
EXPLAIN (COSTS OFF) MATCH (m:movie) FINISH;

        QUERY PLAN
---------------------------
 Limit
   -&gt;  Seq Scan on movie m
(2 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   A write plan keeps its write node, because the write is not optional:
<programlisting>
EXPLAIN (COSTS OFF) CREATE (:movie {title: 'Tenet'}) FINISH;

  QUERY PLAN
--------------
 Graph Create
   -&gt;  Result
(2 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>ORDER BY</command>, <command>SKIP</command>, <literal>OFFSET</literal> and
   <command>LIMIT</command> are clauses in their own right, but none of them can end a query.
   <command>FINISH</command> closes a pipeline that ends on one:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie) ORDER BY m.year LIMIT 3 FINISH;

--
(0 rows)
</programlisting>
   Without the <command>FINISH</command> the same query raises
   <literal>Cypher query must end with RETURN, FINISH or update clause</literal>.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-finish-notes">
  <title>Notes</title>

  <para>
   <command>FINISH</command> is not accepted where a result is required. A
   <literal>CALL { }</literal> body has to project something, so a body that ends in
   <command>FINISH</command> raises
   <literal>CALL subquery must RETURN at least one variable</literal>. A query that ends in
   <command>FINISH</command> cannot be the left operand of <xref linkend="cypher-next"/>
   either. That raises <literal>FINISH cannot be followed by NEXT</literal>.
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>finish</literal> became a reserved keyword in 2.18. It is still accepted unquoted
   as a label name in a pattern, as in <literal>MATCH (n:finish)</literal>, which is what an
   upgraded database needs. It is not accepted as a variable name, as a
   <command>CREATE VLABEL</command> name or as a SQL identifier. Write
   <literal>"finish"</literal> in those places, or rename. Property keys and
   <command>RETURN</command> aliases are not affected.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-finish-compatibility">
  <title>Compatibility</title>

  <para>
   <command>FINISH</command> implements the <literal>FINISH</literal> form of the GQL primitive
   result statement of ISO/IEC 39075:2024, section 14.10. The standard gives it the empty type
   and sets the execution outcome to a successful outcome with an omitted result, which is what
   the empty result above represents. It is supported as the terminal statement of a read query
   and of a data modifying query, where section 13.1 makes it implicit anyway. It is not
   supported as a branch of a set operation, which the standard also forbids in section 14.2,
   syntax rule 5. AgensGraph rejects that as
   <literal>each UNION query must have the same number of columns</literal>.
  </para>

  <para>
   openCypher has no <command>FINISH</command>. There, a read query has to end with
   <command>RETURN</command>, and a write query may end with its write clause. AgensGraph
   accepts that second form too, so <command>FINISH</command> adds the read case rather than
   replacing anything.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

